we just heard from gary neville um i  think you were listening into the  interview  i just want to read what he said to you  in full about  labour abstaining on that vote on covert  restriction tears he said  when you're elected and you're in that  seat in westminster you take a position  you don't abstain you take part in the  match you're the opposition  don't sit in the stand he's right isn't  he  labour did sit on the stand  no i respectfully disagree with that  analysis we didn't sit in the stands at  all what we did  was to act in the national interest we  accepted first of all that there should  be  restrictions that's why we didn't oppose  them  but at the same time there were still  issues  so for example there wasn't a  comprehensive  health and indeed economic strategy  alongside the restrictions there wasn't  that communication strategy  there wasn't the strategy the effective  strategy we need  around test track and isolate and there  were substantial gaps in the economic  package  so in that situation the appropriate  thing to do was not to block the  restrictions altogether that wouldn't  have been in the national interest  but they weren't at the level where we  could give them our wholehearted support  and that's why we took the  decision to abstain it turned out to be  a very important decision  because it seemed in the numbers that  there were sufficient numbers  of conservative mps rebelling that the  prime minister has frankly lost control  of  that would have created a significant  issue you see  this is what i don't understand you say  that you were you know happy that the  legislation went through because  otherwise there wouldn't be the  restrictions in place  so why don't you just vote for it you  know some might say that trying to have  your cake and eat it here you want the  restrictions to go through you  effectively support them despite your  doubts but you also perhaps want to  reserve the right to criticize the  government if it all goes wrong  well no we also want to put pressure on  the government at the same time to  improve  what is on the table and let me just  take that example  of the economic package which clearly  has been a significant problem  for example you have a situation where  businesses are getting  as it were one-off help it doesn't  reflect  the period that areas of the country  are actually under restrictions take  areas of the north and under  restrictions  for a significantly longer period than  other parts of the country  in addition to that we want more  certainty around the furlough scheme  it's good that it was extended albeit at  the 11th hour  but we still have this threat of changes  to the scheme  in january there are also many people  who've been excluded from hell  particularly the self-employed  so we accept the need for restrictions  but we still are pushing for  improvements  because the package particularly the  economic package put forward by the  government  simply doesn't match the ambition of the  moment so you're abstained on the coved  restrictions  are you going to explain if there's a  brexit trade deal vote as well  well on the issue of the brexit  trade deal and potential trade deal my  first message is this  we absolutely need to get a deal  we all know what the consequences of no  deal would be  for the country both in terms of jobs  and livelihoods all across the united  kingdom  but also in terms of that security  partnership that we need  for access to databases and the  operation of international warrants  that allow us to keep our people safe so  absolutely first of all  we need to get a deal and that's the  strong message  in terms of our position on any deal  clearly we need to see what has been  agreed i think that's a sensible  responsible  position to take but let's very much we  hope there is a deal because there seem  to be two paths before us at the moment  a path of leaving the transition period  without a deal  and leaving with a deal and we do know  how catastrophic the no deal outcome  would be  so if a no deal would be catastrophic in  your words  and that the uk absolutely needs to get  a deal  surely the logical conclusion is that  labour is going to vote for a deal  well i think i think there's two points  there i think first of all it's the  responsible thing to actually consider  the deal that is actually agreed but in  addition to that  we don't know at the moment what's  actually going to be presented  to parliament will it be uh the dealers  agreed  will it be a piece of legislation  that is actually giving effect to that  deal in legal terms and we'll have to  uh consider that as well so those are  the questions  that we will as an opposition be  considering and in  considering those we will be putting the  national interest first  and bearing in mind how important it is  that we do actually find a way now of  leaving  with a deal no deal would be  catastrophic at any moment  but it is particularly the case as we go  through this global pandemic  you know listening to your answers you  know on the one hand on the other hand  it feels to me like abstaining is on the  table still  well look it's a question of first of  all  the government we want to get a deal  that is crucially important and that's  why it's been extremely concerning we  want all parties around the table  to reach that deal that's the first  important crucial step  the responsibility is abstaining  well look what i'm saying uh in in terms  of this  sophie is of course there are various  options when you get  any particular vote that you get before  parliament there are the  the options that there always are that's  absolutely the case but what i'm  saying is that the responsible thing to  this is first of all emphasize the need  to get a deal  but then to consider what has been  agreed but also to consider  what's actually going to be put before  parliament it may not be  a straight vote on the deal it may  actually be that time is running out  uh so much now that actually all we'll  get is an actual piece of legislation  putting that deal into effect which of  course we would look at in the way we  look at any piece of legislation  i absolutely understand that of course  you need to see  the legislation but but there is a bit  of a serious point here you know  we know there are incredibly difficult  decisions  on covid and brexit we know that there's  arguments on both sides  but that is why we elect politicians to  make  those difficult decisions you know  you've abstained  on covid it sounds like you might  abstain on brexit as well  well look we consider each particular  circumstance and i've explained why we  took the approach that we did  on the coveted restrictions that were  put before parliament  we will now look at the issue of a  brexit deal  if one is agreed putting the national  interest first which is absolutely what  a responsible opposition  should do we will then consider as i say  what has been agreed we'll then have to  consider in addition  the form in which it is put to  parliament and what's right what we're  actually being  uh asked to do and ensuring as well by  the way  that whatever scenario there is on the  31st of december that the government has  actually prepared for that  and that's something we've been pushing  in recent months and we'll continue to  push the government on as well  okay and now i'm keen to ask you well  i've got you about one other uh story in  the  newspapers this weekend and the  government's trying to deport  up to 50 jamaican nationals some of them  came here as children some of them have  family here  but they have subsequently committed  crimes in the united kingdom  is it right that convicted criminals  born overseas  should be deported  well look labour will always act in the  national  interest and if there are people who are  convicted of very serious crimes who've  lost their right  to remain then yes they should be  deported but the point that we were  raising with regard to that particular  flight  was deep concern around the windrush  scandal  and continuing injustice where of course  people have been  deported who shouldn't have been in the  past and that's why we put forward  as a labour front bench very serious  questions  of the government on the work that they  had done on this flight  for example we've put issues of someone  on the flight having a grandfather from  the windrush generation  a great aunt who may even have been on  the actual empire  wind rush so asking those questions was  extremely important and indeed we seem  to have been vindicated in asking those  questions  when it does appear that significant  numbers of people originally  meant to go on that flight did not in  the event leave on it  you bring up windrush and compare this  to windrush  i just wonder do you think some people  might see that as a bit insulting to  compare  you know the deportation of criminals  including rapists  to the attempted deportation of the  windrush generation didn't commit crimes  they came here to the uk decades ago to  work and to try and rebuild the economy  after the war economy after the war  i don't think that think that at all  there's an issue as to whether or not  there were there were people on the  flight who had eligibility for  the windrush scheme but on the broader  issue of the windrush generation the  government is piling  injustice upon injustice we've had the  promise  that the 30 recommendations in the wendy  williams lessons learned review from  march  are going to actually be implemented not  one of them has been implemented  and the windrush compensation scheme is  frankly an offensive  mess a scheme that was expected to run  into the value of hundreds of millions  of pounds  we were told last week it's paid out to  226 people and around about 2.1 million  pounds now of course  i'm pleased that those 226 people have  received at least some compensation for  the terrible injustice they've suffered  but that is a tiny tiny fraction  of the overall scale of this injustice  that scheme is unsurprisingly losing  confidence and the government  desperately needs to get a grip of it
 

